          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A unique factorization ring is like a unique factorization domain but where we do not require the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            to be an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+elements"} regular elements
            of the commutative ring, since the non-regular elements of a commutative ring are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+divisors"} zero divisors
            of a commutative ring.
          %p
            This becomes important in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            when we have multiple different notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
          %ul
            %li
              %p the non-regular elements being zero,
            %li
              %p
                or in rings with a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness+relation"} tight apartness relation
            %li
              %p
                or in rings with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+relation"} decidable
                tight apartness, where both notions coincide
          %p Instead of having to constantly distinguish between different notions of unique factorization domains, we could simply generalize to unique factorization rings.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in R
            is
            %em regular
            if left multiplication by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            and right multiplication by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            is an injection. We say that an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in R
            is a
            %em unit
            if it is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+element"} invertible
            %em irreducible
            if it can not be represented as a product of two non-units.
          %p
            A commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a
            %strong unique factorization ring
            if every regular non-unit has a factorization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u = r_1 \cdots r_n
            (where
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 1
          %p
            Put differently:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a unique factorization ring precisely when the multiplicative monoid of regular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+ideals"} principal ideals
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            (which is isomorphic to the quotient monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Reg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Reg(R)/R^\times
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Reg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Reg(R)
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+subset+of+regular+elements"} multiplicative subset of regular elements
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^\times
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} freely generated
            by irreducible principal ideals.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unique+factorization+domain"} unique factorization domain
                is precisely a unique factorization ring which is an integral domain; i.e. a commutative ring whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+subset+of+regular+elements"} multiplicative subset of regular elements
                are precisely the non-zero elements.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prefield+ring"} prefield ring
                is a unique factorization ring where every regular element is invertible.
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GCD+ring"} GCD ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+ring"} Euclidean ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prefield+ring"} prefield ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unique+factorization+domain"} unique factorization domain
